---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CTA from '../../components/CTA.astro';
import { splits } from '../../data/splits';
import { dayPlans } from '../../data/dayPlans';
import { comparisons } from '../../data/comparisons';
import { goalPlans } from '../../data/goalPlans';
import { articleLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const freq: Record<string, string> = { 'push-pull-legs': '2× (6-day) / 1× (3-day)', 'upper-lower': '2×', 'full-body': '2–3×', 'bro-split': '1×', 'arnold-split': '2×', 'pplul': '2× (most muscles)' };
const bestFor: Record<string, string> = {
  'push-pull-legs': 'Intermediates who want high volume and can train 5–6 days',
  'upper-lower': 'Most lifters on 4 days; strength and size together',
  'full-body': 'Beginners and anyone training 2–3 days a week',
  'bro-split': 'Lifters who enjoy dedicated muscle days and 5 sessions a week',
  'arnold-split': 'Advanced lifters chasing high-volume, high-frequency training',
  'pplul': 'Intermediates who want near-twice-weekly frequency on five days',
};
const desc = 'Compare every major workout split — full body, upper/lower, PPL, bro and Arnold — by days per week, frequency and difficulty, then use the Split Finder to pick yours.';
---
<Layout
  title="Workout Splits: Find the Best Split for Your Goals | SplitsWorkout"
  description={desc}
  canonical="/workout-splits"
  currentPage="splits"
  ogImage="/og/splits.png"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Workout Splits' }]}
  jsonLd={articleLd({ headline: 'Workout Splits: Find the Right Training Split for You', description: desc, path: '/workout-splits', image: '/og/splits.png' })}
>
<div class="page-header"><div class="page-header-inner">
  <h1 class="page-title">Workout Splits: Find the Right Training Split for You</h1>
  <p class="page-desc">A workout split is how you divide your training across the week. Compare the main options, see which fit your schedule, and take your pick straight into the planner.</p>
  <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:16px;">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/workout-split-finder">Find My Split →</a>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/workout-planner">Build a Workout</a>
  </div>
</div></div>

<div class="seo-page">
  <h2>What is a workout split?</h2>
  <p>A workout split (also called a training split or gym split) is the plan that decides which muscles you train on which days. Instead of training everything every session, you divide the work — by body region, by movement type, or not at all — so that each session is manageable and each muscle gets time to recover before it is trained again.</p>
  <p>Choosing a split is really choosing three things at once: <strong>how often you train each muscle</strong>, <strong>how much you do per session</strong>, and <strong>how many days you need to commit</strong>. There is no universally best option, only the best fit for your schedule, goals and recovery.</p>

  <h2>Compare the main workout splits</h2>
  <div class="seo-scroll"><table class="seo-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Split</th><th>Days / week</th><th>Muscle frequency</th><th>Session</th><th>Level</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {splits.map((s) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/workout-splits/${s.slug}`}>{s.shortName}</a></td>
          <td>{s.daysPerWeek}</td>
          <td>{freq[s.slug] ?? '—'}</td>
          <td>{s.sessionDuration}</td>
          <td>{s.difficulty}</td>
          <td>{bestFor[s.slug] ?? s.description}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="seo-note">Frequency shows how often each muscle is trained per week in the standard version of the split.</p>

  <h2>Choose a split by training days</h2>
  <p>Your available days narrow the choice faster than anything else. Each page below lays out the best structures for that schedule with a sample week.</p>
  <div class="seo-chips">
    {dayPlans.map((p) => <a class="seo-chip" href={`/workout-splits/${p.slug}`}>{p.days}-day split</a>)}
  </div>

  <h2>Choose a split by goal</h2>
  <p>Your goal changes what a good split looks like. These guides give different advice for each one.</p>
  <div class="seo-chips">
    {goalPlans.map((g) => <a class="seo-chip" href={`/workout-splits/${g.slug}`}>{g.h1.replace('Best Workout Split ', '')}</a>)}
  </div>

  <h2>Compare splits head to head</h2>
  <p>Torn between two? These comparisons set out the differences in frequency, recovery and who each one suits.</p>
  <div class="seo-chips">
    {comparisons.map((c) => <a class="seo-chip" href={`/workout-splits/${c.slug}`}>{c.aName} vs {c.bName}</a>)}
  </div>

  <h2>How to choose the right split</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with days per week.</strong> Pick a number you can keep up for months, not the number you can manage this week.</li>
    <li><strong>Match your experience.</strong> Beginners tend to progress well on full body or upper/lower; PPL and Arnold work best once you have a training base.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider session length.</strong> Full body means fewer, longer sessions; body-part splits mean shorter, more frequent ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Think about recovery.</strong> More days means less time between sessions. Sleep, food and stress matter as much as the split itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your equipment.</strong> Most splits adapt to dumbbells and bodyweight, but body-part days lean on machines and cables.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If you would rather not work this out by hand, the <a href="/workout-split-finder">Split Finder</a> scores the splits against your answers, and our guide to the <a href="/workout-splits/best-workout-split">best workout split for you</a> explains the reasoning.</p>

  <CTA variant="generic" />

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="seo-faq">
    <details><summary>How many days a week should I train?</summary><p>Three to four days suits most people. Two days works for a busy schedule and five or six only makes sense if you recover well and want more volume.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How often should I train each muscle?</summary><p>Training each muscle about twice a week is a practical middle ground and easy to organise, but once or three times a week can also work if total weekly volume is sensible.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is it OK to change my split?</summary><p>Yes. Many lifters change split as their schedule, goals or experience change. Give a plan at least 8–12 weeks before judging it.</p></details>
  </div>

  <h2>Related guides</h2>
  <p>Read <a href="/guides/how-many-days-a-week-should-you-train">how many days a week you should train</a> and <a href="/guides/which-muscles-to-train-together">which muscles to train together</a> for the reasoning behind every split.</p>

  <h2>Build, then explore</h2>
  <p>Once you have a split in mind, load it into the <a href="/workout-planner">workout planner</a>, browse the <a href="/exercises">exercise library</a> for each muscle, and use the <a href="/muscles">muscle guide</a> to see how every muscle group is trained. For nutrition targets that match your goal, try the <a href="/calculators/tdee">TDEE calculator</a>.</p>
</div>
</Layout>
